    feat(frontend): ask a stats question from the home page

    The home page is gated on performance, so the only part of this in its bundle is
    a heading and a button: 357 bytes gzipped. The dialog, the contract validation
    and the statistics components it renders are a 3.5 KB gzipped chunk imported when
    the visitor first reaches for the button, on focus as well as click so a keyboard
    visitor does not wait. That follows HomeHero, which loads the Three.js scene the
    same way. The section reserves its height and the dialog is an overlay, so
    neither arriving nor opening shifts anything.

    It answers one question at a time and keeps no transcript, because the endpoint
    behind it keeps no history and an interface that looked like a chat would promise
    one. Each answer leads with the definition the question was read as, so a reader
    sees the interpretation before the figures: the platform published the figures,
    and the reading of the question is the only part they cannot otherwise check.

    Every outcome the endpoint can return has its own message, including all eight
    unsupported reasons, and the four example questions were confirmed against the
    deployed public reads rather than only against the translation evaluation —
    translating a question is not the same as its entities resolving.

    ADR-017 is disclosed before the first question rather than after it.

    Refs #816

